---
/**
 * MCP Tester tool page.
 */
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import McpTester from '../../components/tools/McpTester';
---

<ToolLayout slug="mcp-tester">
  <McpTester client:load />

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      An HTTP client will tell you an MCP endpoint returned 200. It will not tell you whether the
      handshake negotiated the version you thought, whether the tool schemas are shaped so a model
      can actually call them, whether an unknown method is rejected properly, or what the round trip
      costs from where you are sitting. Those are the things that break, and they break quietly.
    </p>
    <p>
      The protocol also moved underneath everyone. Revision <code>2026-07-28</code> removed the
      <code>initialize</code> handshake, protocol-level sessions and the <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code>
      header, replaced the standalone GET stream with <code>subscriptions/listen</code>, and made
      every request carry its own protocol version and client capabilities in <code>_meta</code>.
      Plenty of deployed servers still speak the older shape. A tester that only knows one era is
      wrong about half the servers it meets.
    </p>

    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>
      The probe follows the specification's own compatibility ladder. It opens with a modern
      <code>server/discover</code>, carrying the mirrored <code>MCP-Protocol-Version</code> and
      <code>Mcp-Method</code> headers. On a 400 it reads the body before concluding anything: a recognised
      modern error means the server is modern and something else is wrong. An <code
        >UnsupportedProtocolVersionError</code
      > also names the versions it does support, so the probe retries at one of those instead of falling
      back. Only a body that is not a modern error sends it down to the pre-2026 <code
        >initialize</code
      > path, session header and all.
    </p>
    <p>Then it checks the things a status code hides:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Tool definitions.</strong> Missing <code>inputSchema</code>, a schema that is not an
        object, absent descriptions, duplicate names, and <code>x-mcp-header</code> annotations outside
        the permitted shape. That last one matters because a conforming client must
        <em>drop</em> a tool whose annotation is invalid.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Error handling.</strong> An unknown method must come back as <code>-32601</code>. A
        server that answers 200 to a method it has never heard of will swallow a typo'd tool call
        and surface the failure somewhere else entirely.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cacheability.</strong> From 2026-07-28 list results must carry <code>ttlMs</code> and
        <code>cacheScope</code>. Without them every client re-fetches the tool list on every run.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Latency.</strong> Repeated <code>tools/list</code> calls, reported as a median rather
        than a single sample, so a cold start does not masquerade as steady-state performance.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every check keeps the exact HTTP exchange behind it, viewable and copyable as
      <code>curl</code>. "tools/list failed" is not a bug report; the request that produced it is.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it runs in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      Requests go straight from this tab to the endpoint you name. There is no proxy, which is a
      deliberate choice rather than a missing feature: a hosted proxy that forwards requests to any
      URL a stranger types is an open server-side request forwarder pointed at internal ranges and
      cloud metadata endpoints, and that is not worth shipping to dodge CORS.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cost is that a server without CORS headers cannot be reached from a browser at all. When
      that happens the tool says so precisely and hands over a <code>curl</code> command that will work
      from a terminal. It also means you can probe <code>localhost</code> during development, which a
      hosted proxy could never do.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it stops being trustworthy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A blocked request is opaque by design. CORS rejection, a dead port, a wrong hostname and a
        self-signed certificate all reach script as the same failure. The browser does not say
        which, and neither can this.
      </li>
      <li>
        Latency is measured from your machine over your network. It is the number your browser would
        see, not the server's own processing time.
      </li>
      <li>
        Elicitation, sampling and the multi round-trip pattern are not exercised. A tool call that
        returns <code>input_required</code> is reported as such and not carried further.
      </li>
      <li>
        Tool calls are real calls against a live server. If a tool writes something, it writes it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
